A recompute summary carrying a `product_sha256` that is not a digest. Every
field its kind declares is present and well-formed; only the digest's CONTENT
is wrong, so the row survives the required-field parse and reaches
`admit_summary`, which is where the check lives.

THIS FIXTURE EXISTS BECAUSE THE CHECK HAD NEVER BEEN RED FOR THIS KIND. The
2026-09-11 ruling moved `product_sha256` onto `Event::Summary` and the check
with it, out of the drive arm and in front of the kinds. Nothing proved the
second half. The only recompute fixture touching the digest is
`recompute-summary-without-its-product-digest.jsonl`, and it has the field
MISSING -- caught by `take_string` before `admit_summary` runs at all, so it
cannot tell "the digest's content is checked for every kind" from "the digest
is checked for drives only". A fresh instance narrowed the check back to the
drive arm and watched 597 tests pass over a record whose digest reads
`not-a-valid-digest`.

The seeded fault that was supposed to cover this, `inject_record_recompute_
digest_optional`, changes TWO things at once -- the field becomes optional and
the check narrows to Drive -- and grades on the missing-field fixture, so the
narrowing half rode along untested. Two defects need two fixtures.
